---
import Path from "../guide/Path.astro";
import Page from "../site/Page.astro";
import { leavesSite, REPOSITORY, sitePath } from "../site/links.ts";
---

<Page title="Privacy policy · Katari" description="What Katari sends, where it goes, and how to turn it off.">
  <main data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-filter="section:Privacy">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated 9 November 2026</p>
    <p>
      Katari keeps your library, reading and watching progress, history, downloads, backups and settings on your device.
      It has no accounts, and no server of its own that receives them. This page lists everything that leaves your
      device, where it goes, and how to stop it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="crash-logs-and-usage-data">Crash logs and usage data</h2>
    <p>
      Only Katari and Katari for Linux as released on GitHub send them. <strong>Katari FOSS</strong> and builds made from
      the source code send nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two switches decide what is sent. Setup shows them, and you can change them any time in
      <Path>Settings › Security and privacy</Path>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Send crash logs</strong> is on until you turn it off.</li>
      <li><strong>Allow analytics</strong> is off until you turn it on.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>With both off, Katari sends nothing. Turning a switch off stops what it covers from then on.</p>

    <h3>What they contain</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Crash logs</strong>: the error and where in Katari's code it happened, including when Katari stops
        responding. An error's message can include what Katari was working on, such as a web address from a source.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Usage data</strong>: when Katari is installed, updated, opened and put in the background.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Both</strong>: the screen that was showing and the kind of entry it was about, such as a comic or a
        video, but never its title. Katari's version. Your device's maker and model, system version, language and screen
        size, and on Android its network type and mobile carrier. On Linux, the distribution, the desktop and the
        session type.
      </li>
      <li>
        A random identifier created on your device, so that reports from one installation can be told apart. It is not
        linked to your name, an email address or any account. Clearing Katari's data or installing it again creates a
        new one.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where they go</h3>
    <p>
      Reports go to <a href="https://posthog.com" {...leavesSite()}>PostHog</a>, a product analytics service, and are
      stored in its EU cloud. PostHog works out an approximate location, the country and city, from the IP address a
      report comes from, then discards the address without storing it. <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy" {...leavesSite()}>PostHog's privacy policy</a>
      covers how it handles them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only Katari's developer reads them, to find and fix problems and to learn which parts of the app are used. They
      are not sold, shared with anyone else or used for advertising.
    </p>

    <h2 id="connections-you-choose">Connections you choose</h2>
    <p>Everything else Katari sends goes only where you point it, or to a service you turn on.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Extensions</strong> connect to the sites they read comics, books and videos from, and the extension
        stores you add send their list of extensions. Katari doesn't run those sites; their own privacy policies apply.
        Cookies they set stay on your device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sync</strong> sends your library, progress, history and settings only to the
        <a href={sitePath("docs/sync-server/")}>sync server</a> you run yourself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check for updates on launch</strong>, in <Path>Settings › About</Path>, asks GitHub for the newest
        release when Katari opens and every 6 hours while it stays open. Updates download from GitHub too.
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" {...leavesSite()}>GitHub's
        privacy statement</a> applies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <Path>Settings › Players › Anime player › Find intro and credits times online</Path> is off until you turn it
        on. Then, when a source marks no intro or credits, Katari sends the series title and episode number to the
        services you leave on below it: AniSkip, which it finds the series for through AniList, and TheIntroDB, through
        TVmaze and Wikidata.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Translation</strong> with a <strong>DeepL-compatible server</strong> or a
        <strong>LibreTranslate Server</strong> sends the text to that server. Katari asks you to accept the engine's
        privacy disclosure before it translates anything. The other engines translate on the device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Text recognition</strong> reads pages on the device, with models downloaded once from Hugging Face.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Text to speech</strong> uses the voices your system provides. A network voice sends the text to its
        engine's service.
      </li>
      <li>
        <Path>Settings › Advanced › DNS over HTTPS (DoH)</Path> is off until you pick a provider. Then that provider
        learns the names of the sites Katari connects to.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="this-website">This website</h2>
    <p>
      This website uses no cookies, analytics or trackers. If you pick a theme, your browser remembers it, and it never
      leaves your browser. GitHub hosts the site, and
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" {...leavesSite()}>GitHub's
      privacy statement</a> covers what it logs about visits.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes-and-questions">Changes and questions</h2>
    <p>
      When what Katari sends changes, this page changes with it. Ask about anything here by
      <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/issues`} {...leavesSite()}>opening an issue on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Page>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem var(--gutter) 0;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 3.6rem);
  }

  .updated {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  main p,
  main li {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  main li + li {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
